<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarquinius Priscus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 616–579 BCE (traditional)</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Lucius Tarquinius Priscus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucius Tarquinius Priscus was, according to Roman tradition, the fifth king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, reigning from 616 to 579 BCE. Said to be the son of Demaratus, a Corinthian exile who settled at Tarquinii, he moved to Rome and won the throne after the death of Ancus Marcius. Tradition credits him with laying out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circus-maximus">Circus Maximus</a>, draining the Forum and beginning the Temple of Jupiter on the Capitoline.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, Tarquinius came from the Etruscan city of Tarquinii and was first named Lucumo. Modern scholars note that lucumo was a title of political rank among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, meaning king, so the tradition may have mistaken a title for a personal name. His father Demaratus was a Greek merchant from Corinth, who according to Dionysius of Halicarnassus belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacchiadae">Bacchiadae</a> and left the city when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypselus">Cypselus</a> overthrew them.</p><p>After inheriting his father's fortune, Lucumo was denied office at Tarquinii because his father was Greek-born. His wife Tanaquil, gifted in prophecy, persuaded him to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where foreigners were said to be readily made citizens. Tradition tells that an eagle took his cap on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janiculum">Janiculum</a> and replaced it, an omen she read as a sign of kingship. In Rome he took the name Lucius Tarquinius and became the trusted adviser of King Ancus Marcius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Ancus died his sons were still boys. Tarquinius sent them off on a hunting trip and then won the Comitia Curiata with a speech, becoming the first king in Roman history to canvass for the throne. He is said to have fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>, the Sabines and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s first triumph in Etruscan style, and added a hundred men from lesser noble families to the Senate, bringing it to two hundred.</p><p>The sources also credit him with building. He is said to have laid permanent seating for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circus-maximus">Circus Maximus</a> and to have built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloaca-maxima">Cloaca Maxima</a> to drain the low ground for the Forum. He began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-jupiter-optimus-maximus">Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> and a stone wall around the city, though neither was finished in his reign; the temple was dedicated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a> in 509 BCE. Tradition also gives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the fasces, the curule chair and the toga praetexta from him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to tradition, assassins organised by the sons of Ancus Marcius killed him with a blow of an axe to the head, and Tanaquil is said to have managed the transfer of power so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servius-tullius">Servius Tullius</a> took the throne. Modern historians treat most of the account as foundation legend shaped in a later age; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and Dionysius wrote about five hundred years after the events, relying on annalists who survive only in fragments. The 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica noted that the story of his Corinthian origins is chronologically impossible. Excavations beneath the Forum have found channels thought to belong to an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloaca-maxima">Cloaca Maxima</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucius Tarquinius Priscus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Etruscan king <b>Tarquinius Priscus</b> founded a Capitoline temple to Jupiter, Juno and Minerva, the model for the highest official cult of the Roman world.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greek historian Theopompus, in the 4th century BCE, described their freedom with open hostility, while Roman tradition made Tanaquil, wife of <b>Tarquinius Priscus</b>, a skilled reader of omens who helped to make two kings.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Society</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lucius Tarquinius Priscus</b>, by Roman tradition the fifth king of Rome, celebrated a triumph after defeating the Sabines, who ceded the town of Collatia in the peace that followed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-13">13 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman tradition said Demaratus of Corinth settled here and his son became Rome's king <b>Tarquinius Priscus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tarquinii">Tarquinii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tarquinia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.26,11.8&amp;year=-598" aria-label="Tarquinia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4836 660 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tarquinius Priscus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5286 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5286 930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.26,11.8&amp;year=-598">What was here in 598 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
